---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { products } from '../../data/products';
import type { Product } from '../../data/products';

const slugOrder = [
  'apc-be600m1',
  'apc-bx1500m',
  'cyberpower-cp1500pfclcd',
];

const picks = slugOrder.map(slug => products.find(p => p.slug === slug)!).filter(Boolean) as Product[];
const topPick = picks[0];

const bestFor: Record<string, string> = {
  'apc-be600m1':          'Best Overall — Purpose-Built for Routers and Modems',
  'apc-bx1500m':          'Best for Home Office Networks With Multiple Devices',
  'cyberpower-cp1500pfclcd':'Best for Gaming PCs or Workstations on the Same Circuit',
};

const networkRuntime: Record<string, string> = {
  'apc-be600m1':          '25–35 min at 60–80W network load',
  'apc-bx1500m':          '45–60 min at 60–80W network load',
  'cyberpower-cp1500pfclcd':'60–80 min at 60–80W network load',
};
---

<Layout
  title="Best Battery Backup for Home Network (2025) — Keep Your Router and Modem Online"
  description="The best UPS battery backups for keeping your home router, modem, and network switch online during power outages. APC and CyberPower options compared for home network use."
>
  <div class="bg-amber-50 border-b border-amber-100">
    <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-2 text-xs text-amber-800 text-center">
      This site earns commissions from purchases made through our links at no extra cost to you.
    </div>
  </div>

  <article class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-12">

    <nav class="text-xs text-gray-400 mb-6">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-green-700">Home</a>
      <span class="mx-2">›</span>
      <a href="/backup-power/" class="hover:text-green-700">Backup Power</a>
      <span class="mx-2">›</span>
      Best Battery Backup for Home Network
    </nav>

    <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3 leading-tight">
      Best Battery Backup for Home Network (2025)
    </h1>
    <p class="text-sm text-gray-400 mb-6">Last updated: June 2025</p>

    <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
      A power outage doesn't have to mean losing Wi-Fi. A UPS (uninterruptible power supply) keeps your router, modem, and network switch running for 30–90 minutes after the power goes out — long enough to work through brief outages, make emergency calls, and stay connected on your phone while the lights are off.
    </p>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-8 leading-relaxed">
      Network gear draws very little power — a typical home router, cable modem, and Wi-Fi access point together draw 30–80W. Even a small 600VA UPS can keep all three running for 25–35 minutes. The right UPS for your home network depends on how many devices you need to protect and how long you need to stay online.
    </p>

    <!-- Power Draw Guide -->
    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-2xl p-5 mb-8 text-sm text-blue-900">
      <p class="font-semibold mb-3">Typical home network power draw:</p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="w-full text-xs border-collapse">
          <thead class="bg-blue-100">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-2 py-1.5 text-left border border-blue-200">Device</th>
              <th class="px-2 py-1.5 text-left border border-blue-200">Typical Draw</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr class="bg-white"><td class="px-2 py-1 border border-blue-200">Cable modem</td><td class="px-2 py-1 border border-blue-200">10–15W</td></tr>
            <tr class="bg-blue-50"><td class="px-2 py-1 border border-blue-200">Wi-Fi router</td><td class="px-2 py-1 border border-blue-200">15–25W</td></tr>
            <tr class="bg-white"><td class="px-2 py-1 border border-blue-200">Mesh Wi-Fi satellite</td><td class="px-2 py-1 border border-blue-200">10–20W</td></tr>
            <tr class="bg-blue-50"><td class="px-2 py-1 border border-blue-200">8-port network switch</td><td class="px-2 py-1 border border-blue-200">5–10W</td></tr>
            <tr class="bg-white"><td class="px-2 py-1 border border-blue-200 font-medium">Total typical home network</td><td class="px-2 py-1 border border-blue-200 font-medium">40–70W</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Top Pick -->
    <div class="bg-green-50 border border-green-200 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-10">
      <p class="text-xs font-semibold text-green-700 uppercase tracking-widest mb-2">Top Pick — Best UPS for Home Network</p>
      <p class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-1">{topPick.name}</p>
      <p class="text-gray-600 text-sm mb-3">{topPick.tagline}</p>
      <div class="flex items-center gap-4">
        <span class="text-2xl font-bold text-green-700">${topPick.price}</span>
        <a href={topPick.affiliateUrl} target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener"
           class="bg-orange-500 hover:bg-orange-600 text-white font-semibold px-5 py-2 rounded-md text-sm transition-colors">
          View on Amazon →
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Comparison Table -->
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4">Quick Comparison</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-10">
      <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse border border-gray-200">
        <thead class="bg-gray-100">
          <tr>
            <th class="border border-gray-200 px-3 py-2 text-left">UPS</th>
            <th class="border border-gray-200 px-3 py-2 text-left">Best For</th>
            <th class="border border-gray-200 px-3 py-2 text-left">Capacity</th>
            <th class="border border-gray-200 px-3 py-2 text-left">Network Runtime</th>
            <th class="border border-gray-200 px-3 py-2 text-left">Waveform</th>
            <th class="border border-gray-200 px-3 py-2 text-left">Price</th>
            <th class="border border-gray-200 px-3 py-2 text-left">Link</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {picks.map((p, i) => (
            <tr class={i % 2 === 0 ? 'bg-white' : 'bg-gray-50'}>
              <td class="border border-gray-200 px-3 py-2 font-medium">{p.name}</td>
              <td class="border border-gray-200 px-3 py-2 text-xs text-green-700 font-medium">{bestFor[p.slug]}</td>
              <td class="border border-gray-200 px-3 py-2">{p.specs['Capacity']}</td>
              <td class="border border-gray-200 px-3 py-2 text-xs font-medium">{networkRuntime[p.slug]}</td>
              <td class="border border-gray-200 px-3 py-2 text-xs">{p.specs['Waveform']}</td>
              <td class="border border-gray-200 px-3 py-2">${p.price}</td>
              <td class="border border-gray-200 px-3 py-2">
                <a href={p.affiliateUrl} target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener"
                   class="text-blue-600 hover:underline font-medium">Amazon →</a>
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <!-- Reviews -->
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-6">Full Reviews</h2>

    {picks.map((p, i) => (
      <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-6">
        <div class="flex items-start justify-between gap-4 mb-3">
          <div>
            <div class="text-xs font-semibold text-green-700 uppercase tracking-wide mb-1">
              #{i + 1} — {bestFor[p.slug]}
            </div>
            <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900">{p.name}</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 text-sm mt-1">{p.tagline}</p>
          </div>
          <div class="text-right flex-shrink-0">
            <div class="text-2xl font-bold text-green-700">${p.price}</div>
            <div class="text-xs text-gray-500 mt-0.5">{networkRuntime[p.slug]}</div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-3 text-sm mb-4">
          {Object.entries(p.specs).map(([key, val]) => (
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded px-3 py-1.5">
              <span class="font-medium text-gray-700">{key}:</span> {val}
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>

        <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 mb-4">
          <div>
            <div class="font-semibold text-green-700 mb-1 text-sm">Pros</div>
            <ul class="text-sm space-y-1">
              {p.pros.map(pro => <li class="flex gap-1.5"><span class="text-green-500 mt-0.5">✓</span><span>{pro}</span></li>)}
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div>
            <div class="font-semibold text-red-600 mb-1 text-sm">Cons</div>
            <ul class="text-sm space-y-1">
              {p.cons.map(con => <li class="flex gap-1.5"><span class="text-red-400 mt-0.5">✗</span><span>{con}</span></li>)}
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

        <a href={p.affiliateUrl} target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener"
           class="inline-block bg-orange-500 hover:bg-orange-600 text-white font-semibold px-5 py-2 rounded-md text-sm transition-colors">
          Check Price on Amazon →
        </a>
      </div>
    ))}

    <!-- Buying Guide -->
    <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-8">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4">Home Network UPS Buying Guide</h2>

      <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-800 mb-2">Do You Need Pure Sine Wave for Networking Gear?</h3>
      <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm mb-4">
        No. Routers, modems, and Wi-Fi access points all use switching power supplies that work fine with simulated sine wave UPS output. Pure sine wave matters for gaming PCs and workstations with active PFC (aPFC) power supplies — not networking gear. The APC BX1500M's simulated sine wave is perfectly safe for every router and modem on the market.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-800 mb-2">How Long Will My Network Stay Up?</h3>
      <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm mb-4">
        A typical home network (router + modem + one mesh satellite) draws 40–60W. The APC BE600M1 at 330W capacity can run that load for 25–35 minutes. The APC BX1500M at 900W can run it for 45–60+ minutes. Actual runtime depends on battery age and temperature — a 3-year-old battery delivers about 75% of its original capacity.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-800 mb-2">Should You Protect Just the Network or More?</h3>
      <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm mb-4">
        For the APC BE600M1 at $75, the math is easy: at 40–70W network load, you're only using about 20% of the 330W capacity. You have room to add a NAS drive, a streaming device, or a smart home hub on the same UPS without meaningfully reducing network runtime.
      </p>
      <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm">
        For the larger APC BX1500M or CyberPower CP1500PFCLCD, you can protect your full home office: network gear, two monitors, a non-gaming laptop, and a printer. These are the right choices if you work from home and need 45+ minutes to save work and properly shut down.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Internal Links -->
    <div class="border-t border-gray-200 pt-6">
      <h2 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Related Guides</h2>
      <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm">
        <li>→ <a href="/backup-power/best-ups-battery-backup-home-office/" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">Best UPS Battery Backup for Home Office</a></li>
        <li>→ <a href="/backup-power/best-backup-power-home-office/" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">Best Backup Power for Home Office</a></li>
        <li>→ <a href="/backup-power/best-battery-backup-for-gaming/" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">Best Battery Backup for Gaming Setups</a></li>
        <li>→ <a href="/backup-power/best-portable-power-station-under-500/" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">Best Portable Power Stations Under $500</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>

  </article>
</Layout>
